WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.829 --> 00:00:14.887
The same analysis can run in several data centres. In Stockholm it emits about 14 g of CO2.

2
00:00:16.498 --> 00:00:20.699
In Tokyo the same job emits about 618 g.

3
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:28.759
The aim: whoever picks the lower-emission place should pay less. The question is who covers the difference.

4
00:00:30.379 --> 00:00:39.580
There are four options. Each can be switched on alone or combined. Each was deployed as its own scheme.

5
00:00:41.191 --> 00:00:50.393
Option 1: the provider itself takes 10 % less for low-emission jobs.

6
00:00:52.005 --> 00:00:59.063
Only the provider can set this share. A scheme can only choose whether to pass it on.

7
00:01:00.674 --> 00:01:10.874
Option 2: the portal charges a 10 % fee on every job and waives it for low-emission places.

8
00:01:12.484 --> 00:01:20.543
Note: Stockholm stays at the list price. It is only cheaper than the others, because they pay the fee.

9
00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:32.357
Option 3: high-emission places pay a surcharge into a pool, which pays the discount. No outside money.

10
00:01:33.968 --> 00:01:44.029
Option 4: a funder deposits money in advance, and it pays half of a low-emission job, up to 5 per job.

11
00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:52.991
This table puts the options side by side for the same job: what the user pays, and who covers the rest.

12
00:01:54.611 --> 00:02:05.384
In Stockholm, against a list price of 11.16: option 1 gives 10.04, option 3 gives 8.93, option 4 gives 6.16.

13
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.058
With all four together the user pays 3.91, while a Tokyo job costs 30.35.

14
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:24.870
Every payment stays on the public ledger, with a link to its transaction. A funder can show where its money went.
